WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.933
(exciting music)

00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:06.537
- Water is just such an amazing medium.

00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:08.459
You can just see the crystals and how they

00:00:08.459 --> 00:00:12.149
form in so many different
ways and then take that

00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:15.209
and extrapolate it to these huge cliffs.

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:17.812
What we do here is create spray ice

00:00:17.812 --> 00:00:20.366
and probably make over
300 different climbs.

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:23.116
(exciting music)

00:00:25.438 --> 00:00:28.542
My name is Alexander Bianchi,
and I'm an ice farmer.

00:00:28.542 --> 00:00:31.939
We're down here in the Uncompahgre Gorge.

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:35.549
We have a water system
at the top of the Gorge.

00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:39.401
We use galvanized steel shower heads.

00:00:39.401 --> 00:00:41.623
And it basically just sprays
water out over the cliffs

00:00:41.623 --> 00:00:45.939
and overnight when it's cold,
it forms ice along the cliffs.

00:00:45.939 --> 00:00:48.541
What we really want is
a nice, clean surface

00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:51.043
for the new water to
come down and just keep

00:00:51.043 --> 00:00:53.476
building off of that so
a lot of what we do here

00:00:53.476 --> 00:00:56.366
actually is repel down a lot
of these faces and just try

00:00:56.366 --> 00:01:00.472
to clear that snow off,
ready for the next layer.

00:01:00.472 --> 00:01:03.448
There's about two miles
of ice if you walk the

00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:05.336
bottom of the canyon and yeah,

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:07.728
we farm ice all along the top of it.

00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:09.949
Being an ice farmer is kinda hard work.

00:01:09.949 --> 00:01:12.423
We're gonna open up the south valve.

00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:13.639
Sometimes I think we're no

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:16.325
more than like glorified plumbers.

00:01:16.325 --> 00:01:19.616
Ice farming is and our engineering.

00:01:19.616 --> 00:01:21.374
I mean, the two I think
are always inseparable.

00:01:21.374 --> 00:01:23.594
Ya know, the engineering is the logistics.

00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:25.987
How do we get water
from point a to point b?

00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:28.501
Sometimes if the valves
aren't cracked quite enough,

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:30.597
you get a little bit of a freeze up.

00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:32.235
The water just doesn't
get through the valve.

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:35.295
The remedy is a blow torch.
(flames engulfing)

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:36.639
I mean, on the artistic
side is once we have

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:38.989
this water where we need it, how
can we do something different?

00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:41.335
How can we make something
interesting, ya know,

00:01:41.335 --> 00:01:43.182
that we've never climbed before?

00:01:43.182 --> 00:01:45.904
It's definitely an artistic
process for some of us.

00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:48.292
(exciting music)

00:01:48.292 --> 00:01:51.314
I grew up rock climbing, ya
know, fair weather climber.

00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:55.080
The first time I came out here
was just kind of blown away.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.842
It's so different than rock climbing.

00:01:56.842 --> 00:01:59.863
I mean, you’re climbing a transient medium.

00:01:59.863 --> 00:02:01.585
The rock is always
gonna be there, this ice

00:02:01.585 --> 00:02:04.777
is not always there so
it creates this diversity

00:02:04.777 --> 00:02:07.583
and, um, this intense experience that I really

00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:11.065
don't find in other facets of climbing.

00:02:11.065 --> 00:02:13.207
And that's why I'm proud
to do what I'm doing.

00:02:13.207 --> 00:02:15.957
(exciting music)

